Add an endpoint wrapper and a task function for the {{ source_name }} operation: $ARGUMENTS

Follow the three layers (sdk-core ADR-0001) exactly. The change meets sdk-core's
definition of done,
https://github.com/xippios/sdk-core/blob/main/docs/definition-of-done.md, and its review
checklist, https://github.com/xippios/sdk-core/blob/main/docs/review-checklist.md: step 9
lists what they check in an endpoint.

1. **Read the upstream reference first.** Open `docs/upstream/FETCHED.md` and the file
   that documents this operation. If the snapshot does not cover it, stop and run
   `/refresh-upstream`; never write a wrapper from memory. Note the exact operation
   name, every parameter with its valid values, the response shape, pagination, and any
   publication date or release identifier the response carries.

2. **Models** in `src/xippios/{{ package_dir }}/models.py`: one `UpstreamModel` subclass per
   payload object. Field names are the upstream names in snake_case; where upstream spells
   one differently, accept that spelling with `Field(validation_alias=...)`, never
   `Field(alias=...)`: the adapters serialise by alias, so `alias=` would hand agents
   upstream camelCase rows while tool arguments are snake_case. With `validation_alias`
   the upstream spelling is accepted on input and every output surface stays snake_case.
   Dates become `datetime.date`/`datetime`, numbers become `int`/`Decimal`/`float`,
   upstream enumerations become `StrEnum`, an enumeration of integer ids `IntEnum`
   (conventions §3). No renaming to match another source. An enumeration written by
{% if has_reference_data %}
   hand, for a value set upstream documents and no packaged list renders, lives here
   too, by the family's precedent: `enums.py` is generated whole
   (`/refresh-reference-data`).
{% else %}
   hand, for a value set upstream documents, lives here too, by the family's precedent.
{% endif %}

3. **Endpoint wrapper** in `src/xippios/{{ package_dir }}/api/<wrapper>.py`, or in the
   service's module where operation ids repeat across services and `docs/layout.md`
   keeps one module per service: one async function. The module and the
   function carry the upstream operation name in snake case, from the operation's id in
   the snapshot (an OpenAPI `operationId`, a WADL method id), else from the path, minus a
   controller prefix that every operation of the API shares, so
   `PSDData_GetCommodityDataByYear` is `get_commodity_data_by_year` in
   `api/get_commodity_data_by_year.py`; conventions §2 gives the rule's cases, an id that
   restates the path and a name taken from the path among them. The verbatim id stays in
   the first line of the wrapper's docstring and in the `endpoint=` the wrapper passes, so
   `meta.endpoint` keeps upstream's exact name. Parameters as upstream names them,
{% if tenant_param %}
   snake_cased; it takes `{{ tenant_param }}`, the tenant's name, and calls
   `xippios.{{ import_path }}._client.client_for({{ tenant_param }})`, that tenant's own client, and
   returns models via
{% else %}
   snake_cased; it calls `xippios.{{ import_path }}._client.client` and returns models via
{% endif %}
   `xippios.core.parse(...)`. It calls `get_json`, or `get_answer` where the answer may
   come from the cache, whose `retrieved_at` the task puts into its `ResultMeta`, or
   `get` and then `decode_json` where it needs more of the response, a header say
   (conventions §6). No defaults beyond upstream's own, no composition, no status-code
   checks: upstream's documented error shapes go into `classify` in `_client.py`, an
   error class of the package's own into `errors.py` (conventions §5, §6).

   An operation that upstream serves from another host than `BASE_URL`'s follows the
   rule of one limiter per host (conventions §2, §6). A second base URL on the same host
   goes through {{ '`client_for(' ~ tenant_param ~ ')`' if tenant_param else '`client`' }} by its absolute URL, and `classify`'s path checks name both
   bases. An origin or mirror that the publisher runs itself, where its canonical host
   refuses automated requests, takes that host's pace through the same client, and
   `docs/upstream/` names both hosts; a mirror that anyone else runs is never sent to. A
   host that a dependency sends to is reached through the dependency's tasks alone, never
   by a wrapper here. Any other host gets a client of its own in `_client.py`, its base
   URL and its `RateLimit` beside the first's, and its row in the table of `CLAUDE.md`,
   with its pace and where the pace comes from, in the same pull request.

4. **Task function** in `src/xippios/{{ package_dir }}/tasks.py`: an async function shaped
   like what a person or agent wants (`get_<thing>(...)`, with the parameters a caller
   thinks in), composing wrappers, resolving pagination with `xippios.core.paginate_*`
{% if has_reference_data %}
   and reference lookups, and returning `Result[T]` with full `ResultMeta` (`source`,
{% else %}
   and returning `Result[T]` with full `ResultMeta` (`source`,
{% endif %}
   `endpoint`, `request`, and `published_at` / `vintage` when upstream provides them). Then:
   `get_<thing> = sync(get_<thing>_async)`, add `"get_<thing>"` to `__all__`, and
   re-export it from `__init__.py`.
{% if tenant_param %}
   It takes `{{ tenant_param }}: str`, one tenant, required with no default, and hands it to
   each wrapper it calls: the family check holds every task to it but the tenant list and
   those `NO_TENANT_TASKS` names (conventions §7).
{% endif %}
   A projection the task returns leaves out documented
   personal identifiers (`ssn`, `iban` and their like) unless the task fetches them
   deliberately and says so in its docstring (sdk-core ADR-0017). A list task whose whole
   list an agent needs, longer than the default result cap, gets a cap of its own in
   `MCP_CAPS` (sdk-core ADR-0038). So does a task whose result is one upstream page that
   must return whole, a cursor-paged page with the `next_token` the caller needs for the
   next page or a bounded lookup: a byte bound of its own, the row bound unchanged, and
   where the upstream bills per result, the docstring states the price per result
   (conventions §8; sdk-core ADR-0038, decision 4). A tool whose cut answer a caller can
   continue, where a second run of its task costs little, takes a paged cap,
   `ResultCap(paged=True)`, which gives the tool the adapter's own `offset` and ends the
   note of a cut answer with the call for the next rows; a tool whose second run is dear,
   many upstream requests or a billed page, stays unpaged, and registration refuses a
   paged cap to a write task and to a task that takes a parameter named `offset`. A tool
   whose answer is a series ordered oldest first keeps its newest rows with
   `ResultCap(keep="last")` (conventions §8, sdk-core ADR-0047). A bulk task, one that
   returns a file or a list read from one, is named in `BULK_TASKS` beside it:
   Python-only, no tool and no route (sdk-core ADR-0040 decision 4).

   Where a task meets one of these, it takes core's helper and writes none by hand:
{% if has_reference_data %}
   - a parameter whose name holds `country`, `countries`, `area`, `reporter` or
     `partner`: core's decorator `names_codes` names the codes of each call in
     `meta.names`, `@names_codes(country_code=COUNTRIES)` with the declaration of the
     list that step 5 makes, `PackagedList.codes(...)`, and a parameter whose codes no
     packaged list names, or that takes no code, stands in `UNNAMED_CODES` of its tasks
     module, the task's name to the parameter and the reason; core's family check fails
     a parameter that is neither named nor exempt (conventions §4, sdk-core ADR-0056);
{% else %}
   - a parameter whose name holds `country`, `countries`, `area`, `reporter` or
     `partner`: it stands in `UNNAMED_CODES` of its tasks module, the task's name to the
     parameter and the reason, since the source packages no list that names its codes;
     core's family check fails a parameter that no entry exempts (conventions §4,
     sdk-core ADR-0056);
{% endif %}
   - a `since` that takes back the `meta.vintage` of an earlier call: core's
     `newer_than(since, items, released=..., read=...)` keeps what was released strictly
     later, `read` the reader of the vintage's text, core's `naive_time` for a release
     time printed without a zone, which the task also calls on `since` before its
     request, so an unreadable text is refused before the call; the task sets
     `meta.vintage` and `meta.published_at` from the whole answer (conventions §4,
     sdk-core ADR-0052);
   - an upstream that counts a query's records on request and serves at most a limit per
     call: core's `fetch_counted(count, fetch, limit=..., empty=..., narrow=...)` counts
     first, refuses a count over the limit with `QueryTooLarge` before the data request
     and returns the count, which the task sets as `meta.total`, and `classify` maps
     upstream's own refusal of a query as too large to `QueryTooLarge` (conventions §5,
     sdk-core ADR-0053);
   - a series assembled from one request per period: a ceiling declared once,
     `Ceiling(most, unit=...)`, whose `check` refuses a range over it before the first
     request, and `fetch_periods(periods, fetch, skip=...)`, which sends the requests one
     after another and returns each period with its answer for the task to assemble, the
     vintage included. A lower ceiling on the MCP server takes `mcp=` and `option=`, and
     `tasks.py` gives the tool set the ceiling's `add_options` and `apply_options` as
     `mcp_add_options` and `mcp_apply_options` (conventions §4, sdk-core ADR-0054).

5. **Docstring for agents** on the task: first line one sentence; then an `Args:` block
   explaining every parameter with examples of valid values. The MCP tool description and
   the OpenAPI summary are generated from it. Errors must come from the taxonomy in
   `xippios.core.errors`; raise `NotFoundError` where upstream signals absence
   (conventions §5).
